1
00:00:05,652 --> 00:00:09,334
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know, again, the tradition, it's okay, but in the West, it's the same.

2
00:00:09,874 --> 00:00:10,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

3
00:00:10,374 --> 00:00:18,217
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just in regard to different things, maybe, maybe more specific views, as opposed to, like, genetic tradition, but it's the same.

4
00:00:18,717 --> 00:00:21,439
[SPEAKER_00]: People get very upset, we point out.

5
00:00:22,099 --> 00:00:34,004
[SPEAKER_00]: And the fact that you get upset on account of your dharma view, like, if you don't see that as a big problem, because there's nothing else you can, you can really discuss with that person, it's just

6
00:00:35,245 --> 00:00:39,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I can get upset, you know, you offend me, you kick my doll, you do this, you do that.

7
00:00:39,968 --> 00:00:48,894
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you disagree with my dhamma views, if that bothers me, it means, well, those are not the views of dhamma.

8
00:00:49,975 --> 00:00:52,016
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that simple.

9
00:00:52,217 --> 00:00:59,181
[SPEAKER_00]: But yeah, it's remarkable the extent people... how far people would go, kind of knowing that, but ignoring it.

10
00:01:00,722 --> 00:01:01,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignoring...

11
00:01:03,585 --> 00:01:09,189
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, people hold their views so strong, and in the West, definitely, as much as any place.

12
00:01:09,409 --> 00:01:09,789
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

13
00:01:09,889 --> 00:01:14,292
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I mean, maybe it's a problem with the subjective perspective, attitude.

14
00:01:15,753 --> 00:01:20,456
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe in America, especially, because things like existentialism and so on, it's not...

15
00:01:20,496 --> 00:01:30,603
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it's wasting culture, generally, because... and in this universe, right, the education, mainly, you know, the way you grow up, the way you're taught in schools and stuff, you just end up being

16
00:01:32,066 --> 00:01:46,954
[SPEAKER_00]: And no doubt, involuntarily, they're not aware of it themselves, but you end up being channeled in the direction of that kind of objective, sort of materialistic view, whereby, you know, if you really commit to it, you come to a point where you think your thoughts don't exist.

17
00:01:47,515 --> 00:01:48,715
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not the real thing.

18
00:01:48,755 --> 00:01:53,718
[SPEAKER_00]: The real thing is the only thing that is accessible through five senses, things that you can see.

19
00:01:54,399 --> 00:01:57,740
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were to stop and think about what the senses are, you'll get very confused.

20
00:01:58,281 --> 00:02:00,402
[SPEAKER_00]: So you wouldn't do it, you'll just keep going forward.

21
00:02:01,165 --> 00:02:06,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Into the things, into the science, into the everything, out there in the world, basically.

22
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:09,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is all based on the assumption that the world is there independent of you.

23
00:02:09,512 --> 00:02:09,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

24
00:02:09,753 --> 00:02:13,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is a wrong assumption, because, you know, to have that assumption, you need to exist first.

25
00:02:13,916 --> 00:02:16,358
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a contradicted assumption.

26
00:02:16,398 --> 00:02:19,021
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was the main, like, line of existentialism.

27
00:02:19,041 --> 00:02:21,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Sartre's saying, existence precedes the essence.

28
00:02:21,803 --> 00:02:26,627
[SPEAKER_00]: There's just a big, like, no, whatever, whatever our thinking takes us,

29
00:02:28,306 --> 00:02:32,248
[SPEAKER_00]: We need to come back to this first principle that existence precedes the essence.

30
00:02:32,288 --> 00:02:42,673
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't provide explanation, you can't develop an external view that will sort of, then in return, define your existence or something.

31
00:02:42,713 --> 00:02:42,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

32
00:02:42,994 --> 00:02:45,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you need to exist first in order to do any of that.

33
00:02:45,515 --> 00:02:48,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means that's all secondary, which means you cannot stand for existence.

34
00:02:49,577 --> 00:02:51,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, they couldn't go further than that.

35
00:02:56,462 --> 00:02:59,884
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a huge change that happened to me with Yadavira.

36
00:02:59,984 --> 00:03:01,666
[SPEAKER_02]: It just twisted everything around.

37
00:03:01,686 --> 00:03:05,849
[SPEAKER_02]: Suddenly, these things make more sense to this world.

38
00:03:06,269 --> 00:03:07,350
[SPEAKER_02]: I thought it was so real.

39
00:03:07,530 --> 00:03:11,933
[SPEAKER_02]: And I'm just, you know, a little thing in this world.

40
00:03:12,693 --> 00:03:13,074
[SPEAKER_02]: Real is...

41
00:03:14,094 --> 00:03:16,096
[SPEAKER_02]: I know the world through my own... through experience.

42
00:03:16,156 --> 00:03:20,619
[SPEAKER_02]: So, experience is more fundamental than the objects that I'm...

43
00:03:20,779 --> 00:03:25,262
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm really just creating a model of a world from my experience.

44
00:03:26,493 --> 00:03:27,394
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's determined.

45
00:03:28,074 --> 00:03:37,840
[SPEAKER_00]: In as much as the objects of your senses define the domain of your sense, in that sense, they are also determined by those senses.

46
00:03:38,860 --> 00:03:46,145
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know, if your eyes are not working or limited or whatever, they will limit the whole domain, the whole world of vision.

47
00:03:46,185 --> 00:03:49,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything you've ever seen, everything you will see, everything you see.

48
00:03:50,527 --> 00:03:51,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with any other sense.

49
00:03:52,408 --> 00:03:55,150
[SPEAKER_00]: And it is just that little material

50
00:03:56,255 --> 00:04:03,360
[SPEAKER_00]: organ, sitting in a wall, same as that cup, same as that bin, same as the stone, eyes.

51
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:09,463
[SPEAKER_00]: But as Aniruddha said, quoting that sutta, well, yeah, it's like that.

52
00:04:09,904 --> 00:04:18,529
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just that they have this privileged position of it's an object in the world because of which there are objects in the world, because of which you see objects in the world.

53
00:04:19,290 --> 00:04:20,330
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's an object in the world.

54
00:04:20,931 --> 00:04:21,191
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

55
00:04:23,071 --> 00:04:27,954
[SPEAKER_00]: The practice is really to remove that privilege from your senses, from your sense bases.

56
00:04:29,074 --> 00:04:37,539
[SPEAKER_00]: And which is why, whenever they're described, they're described in that kind of external terms, as a fleshy eye and organs and so on, because that's what they are.

57
00:04:38,780 --> 00:04:39,960
[SPEAKER_00]: They can't be more than that.

58
00:04:42,081 --> 00:04:45,283
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the instrument in the world by which...

59
00:04:45,303 --> 00:04:45,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

60
00:04:46,324 --> 00:04:48,465
[SPEAKER_02]: You are the perceiver and the conceiver of the world.

61
00:04:48,505 --> 00:04:48,725
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

62
00:04:51,023 --> 00:04:53,384
[SPEAKER_02]: I have this weird thing happening in this minute.

63
00:04:53,424 --> 00:04:55,966
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not used to meditating so much, so I don't know.

64
00:04:56,526 --> 00:05:06,931
[SPEAKER_02]: It probably doesn't mean anything, but... last couple of days, like, when my eyes are open and I'm seeing things, it's... sometimes it feels like I'm not looking...

65
00:05:06,951 --> 00:05:12,294
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not seeing objects, I'm seeing, like, images of objects.

66
00:05:12,714 --> 00:05:16,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that can be, I mean, true.

67
00:05:16,816 --> 00:05:18,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a computer screen or something.

68
00:05:18,257 --> 00:05:19,798
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, for as long as

69
00:05:20,929 --> 00:05:33,051
[SPEAKER_00]: When you say you're seeing images of objects, the objects that are now pushed in a background are not regarded as more real or more fundamental than the images of them that you see.

70
00:05:35,652 --> 00:05:41,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the problem, that the assumption can kind of take up anything in that sense.

71
00:05:41,693 --> 00:05:43,473
[SPEAKER_00]: So, oh, these are just images.

72
00:05:43,573 --> 00:05:49,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Or if you say, well, your mind comes first, and people can get a hint of that.

73
00:05:50,075 --> 00:05:55,958
[SPEAKER_00]: That the mind basically determines the experience as such, internal or external, or both.

74
00:05:56,978 --> 00:06:16,527
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, because just in that kind of little taste, little glimpse of that, that insight, so to speak, the assumption takes it out, and then you kind of overshoot the mark, and then you end up, everything is in my mind, like solipsism, no idea, the world is just in me, but you all exist just in my head, kind of thing.

75
00:06:17,160 --> 00:06:27,550
[SPEAKER_00]: But that you can see that being remotely rooted in something that resembles the truth, but actually they overshot the mark and now it's just a just a view that misrepresents.

76
00:06:28,551 --> 00:06:32,215
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you see the images of objects, well, that's what's real for you at the time of seeing.

77
00:06:33,336 --> 00:06:40,803
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the background of the objects just becomes another possibility of those objects.

78
00:06:41,243 --> 00:06:41,704
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words,

79
00:06:42,543 --> 00:06:50,086
[SPEAKER_00]: The objects now, that are pushed to the side, because you've seen the image, become a possibility, a way that those objects can appear again.

80
00:06:50,706 --> 00:06:56,329
[SPEAKER_00]: Because now you see them as an image, then you see them as non-image, you see them as the real object, then they're just their modes of manifesting.

81
00:06:57,049 --> 00:07:00,030
[SPEAKER_00]: And they're all equally real as such in their own domain.

82
00:07:00,851 --> 00:07:06,953
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's not that the real objects are more real than the images, because if you stop and think about it, even the real object is a metal image.

83
00:07:07,754 --> 00:07:08,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Not entirely.

84
00:07:08,834 --> 00:07:10,775
[SPEAKER_00]: There is... it's founded upon the

85
00:07:11,868 --> 00:07:20,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Four great elements, but if that image is not there, you wouldn't be able to even designate it as an object.

86
00:07:20,550 --> 00:07:21,230
[SPEAKER_00]: You just couldn't.

87
00:07:22,910 --> 00:07:27,071
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and that is actually, again, that can be a very good practice.

88
00:07:27,231 --> 00:07:37,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the external things, the things that clearly define, the things that you access through your senses, to start recognizing the mental presence in them.

89
00:07:39,269 --> 00:07:46,252
[SPEAKER_00]: And not just default to an assumption that there are solid objects out there, the way you see them and perceive them.

90
00:07:46,652 --> 00:07:46,812
No.

91
00:07:47,933 --> 00:07:51,014
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't mean that there is a hidden reality behind them.

92
00:07:51,074 --> 00:07:58,357
[SPEAKER_00]: They're real whichever way they appear, but you can't even conceive the idea of appearance if it's not mental.

93
00:07:58,697 --> 00:07:58,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

94
00:07:59,037 --> 00:08:03,979
[SPEAKER_00]: If it doesn't partake, at least to some extent, in a domain of mental and mentality.

95
00:08:04,199 --> 00:08:08,961
[SPEAKER_00]: It has to be the, I don't know, sañña and all that other stuff that comes in.

96
00:08:11,887 --> 00:08:13,148
[SPEAKER_02]: Today I had a strange thing.

97
00:08:14,288 --> 00:08:15,629
[SPEAKER_02]: Meditation has been very mild.

98
00:08:15,649 --> 00:08:18,572
[SPEAKER_02]: I think because tired or something.

99
00:08:18,632 --> 00:08:20,053
[SPEAKER_02]: Today my mind was wandering a bit.

100
00:08:20,613 --> 00:08:24,917
[SPEAKER_02]: And I would have like an image just popped in my head.

101
00:08:24,957 --> 00:08:26,878
[SPEAKER_02]: In this case, it was like a bowl of ice cream.

102
00:08:29,001 --> 00:08:50,557
[SPEAKER_02]: And all at once, all together, the image, intense image, and then sort of the awareness that I was aware of it, and then this flame of desire, real craving, and then pleasure, strong pleasure, and it just sort of flush all at once, and three, four, five seconds later, it just disappeared, you know, and I went on to something else.

103
00:08:50,637 --> 00:08:57,823
[SPEAKER_02]: But that was very interesting, and I started to play with that a little bit, because it was not an overwhelming kind of loss, but

104
00:08:58,819 --> 00:09:13,390
[SPEAKER_02]: The one reason it was interesting is because I could really feel the feeling, the pleasurable feeling, and a lot of it... Well, that was the pure kind of example of delight, sensual delight, which is entirely mental.

105
00:09:14,251 --> 00:09:20,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Your senses, they recognize agreeable and disagreeable, the five senses.

106
00:09:20,335 --> 00:09:26,740
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as I said, they're like five animals, they have their domains, they want to... feeding grounds, how they call them, and they want to go after

107
00:09:27,415 --> 00:09:29,076
[SPEAKER_00]: What they like to eat, so to speak.

108
00:09:29,116 --> 00:09:31,737
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the eyes go out, the pleasant sights...

109
00:09:32,317 --> 00:09:35,578
[SPEAKER_00]: But, that's only to a certain extent.

110
00:09:35,638 --> 00:09:44,822
[SPEAKER_00]: The actual notion of sensuality and the delight in sensual pleasures, that's what keeps you bound to them.

111
00:09:45,582 --> 00:09:48,304
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that the existence of the five senses is the problem.

112
00:09:49,624 --> 00:09:56,067
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and that's when you start to recognize, by seeing their delight, how actually it's the mental

113
00:09:57,125 --> 00:10:00,589
[SPEAKER_00]: Take on pleasures associated with senses.

114
00:10:00,969 --> 00:10:05,153
[SPEAKER_00]: That keeps you kind of enslaved to them, not the actual pleasure.

115
00:10:05,173 --> 00:10:13,202
[SPEAKER_00]: So, arahant could eat an ice cream, his favorite one, but he will not induce that mental delight anymore.

116
00:10:13,502 --> 00:10:17,867
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't mean that he won't feel any pleasure of that pleasant, agreeable taste, but

117
00:10:19,353 --> 00:10:28,377
[SPEAKER_00]: For the arising, for the ceasing, and for the persisting of that pleasure, at any time, he won't feel any delight associated with it.

118
00:10:29,557 --> 00:10:48,945
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's another thing, comes down to what I said, when you have yet to start recognizing the mental image, the presentation, the dhamma, basically, in your thoughts and in things that you access through your senses, because they are determined as such, because of the mind.

119
00:10:49,653 --> 00:11:12,652
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you recognize the image of an ice cream and then the real ice cream, they have in common that they both appeared mentally, in a way, as a presentation of an ice cream, as an image of an ice cream, as a phenomenon of an ice cream.

120
00:11:13,272 --> 00:11:14,933
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where the practice needs to pertain.

121
00:11:14,953 --> 00:11:21,817
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it can't pertain just to you, generally, like dismissing your thoughts, or just thinking, or thinking this, you know, in response to that, or whatever.

122
00:11:22,257 --> 00:11:26,500
[SPEAKER_00]: Nor can it pertain only to that which concerns the senses.

123
00:11:26,540 --> 00:11:28,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, no, just say no to everything.

124
00:11:28,261 --> 00:11:31,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Just do not engage in any agreeable sensual

125
00:11:32,107 --> 00:11:36,929
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, sense experience, it's not necessarily sensual, because it will miss the point.

126
00:11:37,550 --> 00:11:48,294
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you free your mind on the level of that mental presentation, that dhamma, that phenomenon, that's in common to both, then you'll be free from both, whichever it is.

127
00:11:48,314 --> 00:11:59,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you think about ice cream, or you're actually eating the ice cream, it will not induce delight, which is that which keeps you bound, which is why its sensuality is unwholesome.

128
00:12:03,073 --> 00:12:07,015
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because usually people associate... ah, thinking is the problem.

129
00:12:07,855 --> 00:12:09,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when I think about it, I get all wound up.

130
00:12:10,156 --> 00:12:11,257
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'll stop thinking about it.

131
00:12:11,917 --> 00:12:16,059
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, yeah, you might do that once, but you won't be always able to stop thinking about it.

132
00:12:17,019 --> 00:12:21,781
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when you do come across ice cream, you just... well, there won't be much restraint.

133
00:12:22,422 --> 00:12:29,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you're restrained, even if you're just moderate and you just eat one spoon a day, still, you eat that one spoon a day with delight.

134
00:12:31,501 --> 00:12:35,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you eat no spoons at all, you will have delight in the earth.

135
00:12:35,562 --> 00:12:39,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why asceticism, per se, is wrong.

136
00:12:39,862 --> 00:12:49,624
[SPEAKER_00]: When people just... the simile of the deer, the herd, in Majjhima Niṭṭhā, I think in the beginning, fourth, fifth sutta, when it says, eh...

137
00:12:52,045 --> 00:12:57,386
[SPEAKER_00]: The first group of deer eats the bait, the mara leaves, unwary.

138
00:12:57,686 --> 00:12:58,386
[SPEAKER_00]: They just eat it.

139
00:12:58,986 --> 00:12:59,806
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't think, well,

140
00:13:00,486 --> 00:13:02,227
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that mother does with them what he wants.

141
00:13:03,127 --> 00:13:16,791
[SPEAKER_00]: The second group of deer, they see the first group and then they blame the bait, so then they completely shun the bait and they run into the mountains where there's no bait around to provoke them.

142
00:13:17,731 --> 00:13:24,713
[SPEAKER_00]: But after a while, their energy, you know, goes down, not enough food, then they come back and they eat the bait unwarily.

143
00:13:25,375 --> 00:13:32,120
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the third group sees the first group, sees the second group, and says, well, what if we stay among the bait, but eat it carefully?

144
00:13:33,281 --> 00:13:41,948
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, they do that, and then the mara, the hunter, comes, and he sees the bait eaten, but nothing is caught on the bait.

145
00:13:42,709 --> 00:13:45,331
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he can't see those deer anymore.

146
00:13:45,591 --> 00:13:49,034
[SPEAKER_00]: They come and eat his bait, but he can't catch them.

147
00:13:49,214 --> 00:13:53,077
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, then it says in the sutra, so then mara decides to leave them alone.

148
00:13:53,777 --> 00:13:59,920
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, try to harass him further, in case he harasses him further, they'll start telling everybody else how to avoid his bait.

149
00:14:02,040 --> 00:14:10,024
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's a comparison to, kind of, obviously, sensual indulgence and then the form of asceticism, which is, kind of, a default mode for many monks, many people.

150
00:14:10,644 --> 00:14:12,845
[SPEAKER_00]: All this bother me, you know, just, kind of, determination.

151
00:14:12,885 --> 00:14:14,125
[SPEAKER_00]: I just say, you know, flirt with it.

152
00:14:14,666 --> 00:14:16,126
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can spend your whole life doing it.

153
00:14:16,546 --> 00:14:17,787
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know, you might not be, kind of,

154
00:14:21,239 --> 00:14:27,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Going around naked, like those ascetics, or wearing a woodbag, or sleeping in thorns, and torturing yourself.

155
00:14:27,502 --> 00:14:34,726
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that your mental attitude denies the bait, blames the bait, means you're not escaping it.

156
00:14:34,767 --> 00:14:35,767
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you're still hooked on it.

157
00:14:35,787 --> 00:14:36,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

158
00:14:37,848 --> 00:14:44,792
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's impossible, really, for anyone in our hunt, as well, to... You can't not experience pain and pleasure.

159
00:14:44,852 --> 00:14:45,393
[SPEAKER_00]: That's true.

160
00:14:45,793 --> 00:14:47,034
[SPEAKER_00]: And so that's what I mean.

161
00:14:48,739 --> 00:14:57,681
[SPEAKER_00]: The wisdom of an Arahant is found in a completely different direction, or in the middle direction, which is basically saying yes to debate, saying no to debate.

162
00:14:57,841 --> 00:15:01,082
[SPEAKER_00]: What does it have in common, any of these reactions?

163
00:15:02,262 --> 00:15:05,343
[SPEAKER_00]: It has in common the phenomenon of debate.

164
00:15:07,183 --> 00:15:17,526
[SPEAKER_00]: If I free my mind in regard to the phenomenon of debate, if I see it as impermanent, as disagreeable, and as something that cannot be appropriated, and practice that long enough,

165
00:15:18,293 --> 00:15:21,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you're among the bait or without the bait, you'll be fine.

166
00:15:21,836 --> 00:15:22,977
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that was never the problem.

167
00:15:23,177 --> 00:15:26,380
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

168
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:29,082
[SPEAKER_01]: I always say, that's what delight is.

169
00:15:30,183 --> 00:15:33,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking that it's... Well, delight, you can... it's actually... it's delight.

170
00:15:34,427 --> 00:15:39,651
[SPEAKER_00]: Delighting in the idea of engaging in something with your senses.

171
00:15:40,732 --> 00:15:43,495
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's a pleasant taste, pleasant sight, pleasant

172
00:15:44,264 --> 00:15:44,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Delight.

173
00:15:44,904 --> 00:15:48,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, allowing your mind to be overtaken by that, means you're delighting.

174
00:15:48,447 --> 00:15:55,511
[SPEAKER_00]: You're falling into it, which means... Well, it means you've been... you're hooked onto the bait.

175
00:15:58,333 --> 00:16:04,657
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but if... if you remove the delight, the bait can stay.

176
00:16:05,337 --> 00:16:06,678
[SPEAKER_00]: It can be right in front of your face.

177
00:16:07,238 --> 00:16:08,899
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot pressure it into biting it.

178
00:16:11,841 --> 00:16:13,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you... you remove that by...

179
00:16:14,973 --> 00:16:15,773
[SPEAKER_01]: By understanding objects?

180
00:16:16,654 --> 00:16:17,394
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

181
00:16:17,634 --> 00:16:18,495
[SPEAKER_00]: By understanding both.

182
00:16:19,255 --> 00:16:19,815
[SPEAKER_00]: Your sense?

183
00:16:20,416 --> 00:16:28,360
[SPEAKER_00]: By understanding the phenomenon that's in common to the objects, whether externally, internally, or internally and external.

184
00:16:31,321 --> 00:16:32,321
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding what appears?

185
00:16:33,782 --> 00:16:34,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Phenomenologically, yes.

186
00:16:36,143 --> 00:16:42,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Not explaining it in terms of, oh, this is all impermanent, this ice cream, you know, it's gonna melt, it's gonna disappear in the sun, it was never real.

187
00:16:43,187 --> 00:16:43,407
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

188
00:16:43,954 --> 00:17:01,106
[SPEAKER_00]: An image of an ice cream, an image of a pleasant taste, an image of a pleasant sight, pleasant touch, that enchants, that has in common, in your mind, with the actual image, even if you're not actually looking at somebody or something, eating something, those things are...

189
00:17:01,126 --> 00:17:07,130
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't need exposure to sensuality in order to define sensuality, because it's defined by your mind.

190
00:17:08,451 --> 00:17:13,435
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you actually need withdrawal from it in order to see that, like the main

191
00:17:14,143 --> 00:17:19,887
[SPEAKER_00]: The main reason for being sensually bound is your mind, your mental assumption in regard to it.

192
00:17:21,088 --> 00:17:24,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you approve that and then it's fine, even if you have things right in front of you.

193
00:17:32,457 --> 00:17:42,765
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it comes down to being able to either think or directly experience through your senses a pleasant thing and not be taken by it.

194
00:17:44,003 --> 00:17:57,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Being able to think or directly experience your senses unpleasant thing, and not feel aversion towards it, in terms of this kind of pressing need to get rid of it.

195
00:17:57,610 --> 00:18:08,035
[SPEAKER_00]: And extends down to what we spoke before about the neutral feeling, which is that where people... well, people don't see it.

196
00:18:08,695 --> 00:18:12,197
[SPEAKER_00]: So, usually that's what's associated with boredom or with indolence.

197
00:18:12,953 --> 00:18:17,095
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need excitement, or you need... well, you need anything, really.

198
00:18:17,896 --> 00:18:21,558
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you don't feel anything, you don't want to be resting on that feeling either.

199
00:18:23,939 --> 00:18:25,440
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's the important bit.

200
00:18:25,460 --> 00:18:26,401
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the subtle one.

201
00:18:27,922 --> 00:18:29,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's a form of delight.

202
00:18:32,324 --> 00:18:32,764
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I...

203
00:18:32,884 --> 00:18:33,965
[SPEAKER_00]: I earned this rest.

204
00:18:34,946 --> 00:18:35,386
[SPEAKER_00]: You did.

205
00:18:36,306 --> 00:18:42,510
[SPEAKER_00]: You might have earned physical rest, but you've never earned, unless you're an arahant, a mental rest.

206
00:18:44,216 --> 00:18:48,459
[SPEAKER_00]: And by mental rest, I mean giving in.

207
00:18:49,240 --> 00:18:58,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, when the Buddha gave that simile of a... of a cow with open wounds, that if it lays on the ground, it's eaten by the insects on the ground.

208
00:18:58,766 --> 00:19:02,669
[SPEAKER_00]: If it lays... if it leans on the tree or a wall, it's eaten by the insects that live there.

209
00:19:03,609 --> 00:19:09,674
[SPEAKER_00]: So, wherever... wherever he chooses to rest just for a second, it's being eaten by that.

210
00:19:10,054 --> 00:19:11,495
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the attitude you need to develop.

211
00:19:12,018 --> 00:19:13,038
[SPEAKER_00]: Mentally.

212
00:19:13,058 --> 00:19:22,482
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, people, again, they overshoot the assumption, the mind, and you end up thinking it's just this constant tension that you have to live through.

213
00:19:22,662 --> 00:19:23,182
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not.

214
00:19:23,602 --> 00:19:30,824
[SPEAKER_00]: You can set your body to sleep, to feed it, take it for a walk, send it for a swing, whatever.

215
00:19:31,325 --> 00:19:34,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Mentally, don't rest upon any of those actions.

216
00:19:36,246 --> 00:19:40,688
[SPEAKER_00]: And don't don't don't rest means don't lose mindfulness in regard to it, basically.

217
00:19:40,708 --> 00:19:40,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't

218
00:19:42,883 --> 00:19:49,945
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't let your mind overtake it, and you forget about yourself, and then you really are taken by what you're doing, even if it's not unwholesome.

219
00:19:50,806 --> 00:19:52,166
[SPEAKER_00]: It's unwholesome for that reason.

220
00:19:54,687 --> 00:19:56,167
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you are proved that you're like.

221
00:19:56,887 --> 00:20:10,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you can notice, if your mind remains unwavered in regard to that subtle, neutral feeling, doesn't need anything, doesn't require anything, doesn't run away from anything, then when pressure arises, you'll be able to leave it alone.

222
00:20:14,359 --> 00:20:20,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the pleasure of the senses, the delight, is mental in regard to the sensual pleasure.

223
00:20:21,564 --> 00:20:34,012
[SPEAKER_00]: But one thing you can recognize is that no matter how great that sensual pleasure might be, it can never ever be anything more than what's defined by your sense organs.

224
00:20:35,753 --> 00:20:36,994
[SPEAKER_00]: It can be the greatest taste.

225
00:20:38,114 --> 00:20:39,275
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still a taste.

226
00:20:39,996 --> 00:20:41,577
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still dependent on the karmic.

227
00:20:42,397 --> 00:20:43,638
[SPEAKER_00]: And it can never be more than that.

228
00:20:44,811 --> 00:20:47,111
[SPEAKER_00]: It can be the most beautiful sight in the world.

229
00:20:47,551 --> 00:21:05,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, when you forget that it's fully determined by the pair of fleshy eyes, you can have the whole image and the drama and the apprehension, the expectation and the realization of this great, you know, spectacle, because you lost sight of yourself and you're completely taken by it.

230
00:21:05,835 --> 00:21:14,176
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you know that whatever you see, whatever you hear, whatever you taste, touch, smell, it can never be more than that.

231
00:21:16,027 --> 00:21:21,111
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in seen, let there just be seen, in heard, let there just be heard, and so on.

232
00:21:21,211 --> 00:21:23,273
[SPEAKER_00]: That's basically what it is.

233
00:21:23,313 --> 00:21:41,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Comes down when you recognize that, again, the entire spectrum of of sense objects, no matter how great it is, left, right, up, down, any direction, it's fully determined and cannot kind of step outside even a millimeter out of the sense organ domain.

234
00:21:42,355 --> 00:21:45,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is a six sense base, which some of the translations are like the fields.

235
00:21:46,677 --> 00:21:48,538
[SPEAKER_00]: The senses are like the fields for these things.

236
00:21:48,978 --> 00:21:50,459
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot be more than that, ever.

237
00:21:51,699 --> 00:21:52,379
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

238
00:21:54,880 --> 00:21:57,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I mean, if I...

239
00:21:57,662 --> 00:22:02,584
[SPEAKER_00]: But not knowing there cannot be more than that, you delight at the prospect of being more.

240
00:22:04,905 --> 00:22:05,545
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's mental.

241
00:22:06,526 --> 00:22:12,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that delight can pull you, push you, make you pursue the senses,

242
00:22:12,923 --> 00:22:16,566
[SPEAKER_00]: But in the end, all you're going to get is sense and its object.

243
00:22:17,687 --> 00:22:23,651
[SPEAKER_00]: And I agree with... which is why usually people out there giving in to desire is like, oh, was that all?

244
00:22:25,673 --> 00:22:30,276
[SPEAKER_00]: The expectations are always higher because that's... that's how the mind... that's how the life works.

245
00:22:31,477 --> 00:22:34,379
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's nothing unusual about it.

246
00:22:34,459 --> 00:22:35,500
[SPEAKER_00]: It's actually quite normal.

247
00:22:36,078 --> 00:22:43,703
[SPEAKER_00]: But, obviously, not knowing that, you associate that delight, that expectation, with this prospect of that sensual desire, a fulfillment.

248
00:22:44,424 --> 00:22:51,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see that that delight takes you in the domain of that sense that has its own scope.

249
00:22:51,228 --> 00:22:55,872
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eyes cannot experience anything more than sights.

250
00:22:57,673 --> 00:23:01,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Ears cannot get you anything more than pleasure of a sound.

251
00:23:02,596 --> 00:23:04,077
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the greatest, the most refined?

252
00:23:04,097 --> 00:23:05,378
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still a sound.

253
00:23:05,981 --> 00:23:14,743
[SPEAKER_01]: The thing of pleasure, the feeling perception, by... if you think ice cream, there's a pleasant feeling.

254
00:23:16,644 --> 00:23:20,284
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, every time you think ice cream, there's a feeling, every single time.

255
00:23:22,725 --> 00:23:28,866
[SPEAKER_01]: That seems to suggest, in my mind, that there is, you know, there is something there.

256
00:23:29,527 --> 00:23:31,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the pleasure is real.

257
00:23:31,968 --> 00:23:37,049
[SPEAKER_01]: But why does it... why is it always connected with any particular perception or image?

258
00:23:37,589 --> 00:23:47,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it can be random, it can be circumstantial, because you try and ask you and you really like it, because there is such a thing as a pleasant taste by default.

259
00:23:47,671 --> 00:23:58,533
[SPEAKER_00]: But the problem is, your question, why is this connected, assumes that the connection between the two is somewhere... somewhere inside of the sense objects.

260
00:23:59,076 --> 00:24:05,338
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you, kind of, unravel it all, then you will be able to, kind of, cut those wires and it will not be... that's not good.

261
00:24:07,039 --> 00:24:17,602
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem... the problem... well, the question you ask is... is a symptom that you are already affected by those... by... by... by the perception of ice cream.

262
00:24:18,462 --> 00:24:22,524
[SPEAKER_00]: It's your response to the fact that you don't know the escape from the ice cream.

263
00:24:23,104 --> 00:24:25,565
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know gratification in that regard.

264
00:24:25,685 --> 00:24:28,906
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't recognize the danger and you don't see the escape from it.

265
00:24:29,900 --> 00:24:30,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, why?

266
00:24:30,680 --> 00:24:31,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, yes.

267
00:24:31,320 --> 00:24:31,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, no.

268
00:24:31,901 --> 00:24:32,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, maybe.

269
00:24:32,601 --> 00:24:33,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, yes and no.

270
00:24:34,141 --> 00:24:43,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Any of those acts are just response... your ways of responding to the fact that you're already touched by that... by that phenomenon you're taking back.

271
00:24:48,866 --> 00:24:53,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Because whatever... whatever is perceived, there is a feeling.

272
00:24:54,428 --> 00:24:56,448
[SPEAKER_01]: There's always a feeling.

273
00:24:56,468 --> 00:24:57,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling and perception.

274
00:24:57,488 --> 00:24:57,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

275
00:24:57,929 --> 00:24:58,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

276
00:24:58,669 --> 00:24:59,009
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

277
00:25:05,253 --> 00:25:06,193
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're trying to undo it.

278
00:25:06,213 --> 00:25:09,035
[SPEAKER_00]: You're trying to separate them, so you're not affected by it anymore.

279
00:25:09,875 --> 00:25:11,676
[SPEAKER_00]: The thing is, you have to go the other way.

280
00:25:13,957 --> 00:25:20,319
[SPEAKER_00]: And by the other way, I mean, by seeing, perception and feeling are inseparable.

281
00:25:21,740 --> 00:25:29,143
[SPEAKER_00]: And if they arise on their own, means, well, you can wonder for the rest of your life, why is the thought of ice cream always pleasant?

282
00:25:31,504 --> 00:25:33,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, you can just accept that,

283
00:25:35,362 --> 00:25:41,105
[SPEAKER_00]: And instead of questioning that, try to find a place whereby you don't care.

284
00:25:42,546 --> 00:25:56,933
[SPEAKER_00]: And by that, I mean, you don't care, not in a sense you ignore it, but in a sense, perception of pleasant ice cream doesn't bother me anymore, doesn't touch me anymore, doesn't command me anymore, doesn't make me do things anymore, doesn't make me think things anymore.

285
00:25:59,154 --> 00:26:00,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Cannot overtake me.

286
00:26:00,655 --> 00:26:00,975
[SPEAKER_00]: And then,

287
00:26:01,656 --> 00:26:05,638
[SPEAKER_00]: You might wonder why is it pleasant, or you might not wonder that, but that would be irrelevant.

288
00:26:05,658 --> 00:26:11,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the only reason you're wondering that now, is because you do realize, you do recognize the problem of being pulled by your senses.

289
00:26:12,542 --> 00:26:15,304
[SPEAKER_00]: So your question is kind of trying to solve it.

290
00:26:16,044 --> 00:26:20,807
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you actually solve it the proper way, questions become redundant.

291
00:26:23,049 --> 00:26:24,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not why is it always pleasant.

292
00:26:25,010 --> 00:26:30,593
[SPEAKER_00]: You should really ask yourself, why, why am I being touched by that, by that image?

293
00:26:31,347 --> 00:26:35,549
[SPEAKER_00]: By that pleasure, why am I being... why is it controlling me?

294
00:26:39,411 --> 00:26:49,216
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it better if I'm... well, if I get over it, so to speak, why is it better if... if I find a way where that pleasure cannot access me?

295
00:26:50,377 --> 00:26:53,739
[SPEAKER_00]: You think about that, and you will start to recognize the danger.

296
00:26:54,599 --> 00:26:56,260
[SPEAKER_00]: You will start to recognize the gratification.

297
00:26:56,300 --> 00:26:58,061
[SPEAKER_00]: And gratification is what I was speaking about.

298
00:26:58,882 --> 00:27:04,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Not loving gratification means confusing your delight with the actual, like, sensual consumption.

299
00:27:05,904 --> 00:27:12,265
[SPEAKER_00]: And you recognize that no matter how great the delight is, gratification remains confined to the domain of a particular sense.

300
00:27:12,725 --> 00:27:19,587
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot... it's inconceivable for it to be anything more, despite what, you know, your delight makes you expect.

301
00:27:21,547 --> 00:27:24,768
[SPEAKER_00]: The pleasure of a sight cannot be experienced by anything other than your eyes.

302
00:27:26,598 --> 00:27:29,700
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize, ah, that's the extent of gratification.

303
00:27:29,840 --> 00:27:32,862
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no more.

304
00:27:32,882 --> 00:27:34,503
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you recognize the danger as well.

305
00:27:36,445 --> 00:27:37,185
[SPEAKER_00]: Expecting more.

306
00:27:37,566 --> 00:27:44,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, through that expectation of more, you blindly jump back into those domains that are not yours.

307
00:27:46,111 --> 00:27:47,793
[SPEAKER_00]: You're going between the eye and sight.

308
00:27:47,833 --> 00:27:52,236
[SPEAKER_00]: You're going between the nose and smells, ears and sounds.

309
00:27:52,636 --> 00:27:55,698
[SPEAKER_00]: And, basically, it's just like running in the middle of a motorway.

310
00:27:57,500 --> 00:27:58,801
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is not yours, but you don't know it.

311
00:27:58,841 --> 00:28:02,062
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just run right in the middle, and then something will hit you sooner or later.

312
00:28:03,323 --> 00:28:11,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is what the Buddha compared killing into sensuality like putting your head on a chopping block, where the axe falls, and it will fall.

313
00:28:13,247 --> 00:28:13,847
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not yours.

314
00:28:14,388 --> 00:28:16,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Neither that perception, nor that feeling.

315
00:28:17,629 --> 00:28:18,650
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're wondering, why?

316
00:28:18,810 --> 00:28:19,950
[SPEAKER_00]: How did that come about?

317
00:28:19,970 --> 00:28:22,911
[SPEAKER_00]: It means you already took it as mine, which that's where the mistake is.

318
00:28:23,612 --> 00:28:26,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Not finding out the answer to those particular questions.

319
00:28:30,541 --> 00:28:34,723
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't help, you can't just stop and will the opposite.

320
00:28:34,883 --> 00:28:36,764
[SPEAKER_00]: I will not engage in sensuality anymore.

321
00:28:38,325 --> 00:28:40,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I guess you could do that if you develop jhānas.

322
00:28:40,786 --> 00:28:44,867
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, through them, you could think that, and then you could stand for that determination.

323
00:28:44,907 --> 00:28:51,290
[SPEAKER_00]: But otherwise, you have to recognize gratification, the danger.

324
00:28:51,530 --> 00:28:54,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it dangerous if I give in?

325
00:28:56,653 --> 00:28:59,274
[SPEAKER_00]: And that will offer the the site of escape.

326
00:29:00,388 --> 00:29:10,556
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, if I remove the delight in this... not in regard to the whole world, just in this particular sense, in these five senses, you're not chasing after every object.

327
00:29:11,056 --> 00:29:16,300
[SPEAKER_00]: You remove the delight in the eye, because of which you delight in the sights.

328
00:29:17,721 --> 00:29:28,469
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you recognize it's fully confined to your eyes, fully confined to your ears, you remove delight in regard to that, and you go, okay, I am free from this, because no matter how much it arises,

329
00:29:29,880 --> 00:29:35,821
[SPEAKER_00]: It can never, sort of, well, go behind me and, kind of, scoop me out again, as it used to do.

330
00:29:37,301 --> 00:29:50,904
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why there's a simile in the suttas, when the Buddha compares a... well, somebody who's free from sensuality, lives like a... like a drop of water on a plate, on a sharp plate, but it's not cut by it.

331
00:29:52,464 --> 00:29:58,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the five senses, basically, entirely, which means your whole world, really,

332
00:29:59,388 --> 00:30:00,449
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not actually yours.

333
00:30:00,469 --> 00:30:01,329
[SPEAKER_00]: It belongs to the mother.

334
00:30:02,771 --> 00:30:06,353
[SPEAKER_00]: The eyes in your head, ears, nose, tongue, body, everything.

335
00:30:07,454 --> 00:30:09,916
[SPEAKER_00]: And what it sees, hears, touches, smells, tastes, and so on.

336
00:30:10,697 --> 00:30:11,277
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not yours.

337
00:30:12,718 --> 00:30:13,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Never was.

338
00:30:15,560 --> 00:30:21,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's it again.

339
00:30:21,545 --> 00:30:22,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Mara is the determinant.

340
00:30:22,645 --> 00:30:28,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, those things are, in and around, undetermined.

341
00:30:30,752 --> 00:30:35,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the determinations have ceased.

342
00:30:35,733 --> 00:30:37,594
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't become the undetermined.

343
00:30:37,614 --> 00:30:40,395
[SPEAKER_00]: They just cease to act as determinations.

344
00:30:40,415 --> 00:30:43,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Determinations of his self.

345
00:30:58,981 --> 00:31:02,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is just another way of saying, he stops taking it as his.

346
00:31:02,542 --> 00:31:03,362
[SPEAKER_00]: And they have ceased.

347
00:31:04,162 --> 00:31:05,943
[SPEAKER_00]: He left... he leaves them be.

348
00:31:07,403 --> 00:31:07,983
[SPEAKER_00]: They've ceased.

349
00:31:08,323 --> 00:31:11,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Ceased to exist as me and mine, and determining me and mine.

350
00:31:15,185 --> 00:31:16,986
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's what the undetermined is.

351
00:31:17,266 --> 00:31:21,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if there wouldn't be undetermined, freedom from determined would not be possible.

352
00:31:21,947 --> 00:31:25,688
[SPEAKER_00]: But because there is the undetermined, freedom from determined is possible.

353
00:31:26,972 --> 00:31:28,633
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the ādhāna doesn't create nibbāna.

354
00:31:28,713 --> 00:31:33,656
[SPEAKER_00]: It just kind of finds a way to step into it, because it's already there, the undetermined.

355
00:31:34,677 --> 00:31:37,879
[SPEAKER_00]: And you step to it, through developing full knowledge of it.

356
00:31:38,760 --> 00:31:47,125
[SPEAKER_00]: There's absolutely no other way.

357
00:31:47,225 --> 00:31:55,771
[SPEAKER_01]: Another question that was sort of related to this, was probably the wrong question, but... that everything seems to...

358
00:31:59,490 --> 00:32:13,334
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, it corresponds.

359
00:32:13,414 --> 00:32:14,194
[SPEAKER_01]: I can feed it.

360
00:32:14,434 --> 00:32:15,515
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, I can touch it.

361
00:32:17,315 --> 00:32:19,036
[SPEAKER_01]: You can experience it through different senses.

362
00:32:19,696 --> 00:32:19,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

363
00:32:20,556 --> 00:32:23,457
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they correspond.

364
00:32:24,717 --> 00:32:28,058
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's very easy to assume that it's one thing out there.

365
00:32:29,167 --> 00:32:30,367
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Indriya said.

366
00:32:30,827 --> 00:32:40,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Either things usually appear as a self, when it's seen twice, either in reflection or experienced simultaneously through two different senses or more.

367
00:32:41,989 --> 00:32:43,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, then they're confused.

368
00:32:43,109 --> 00:32:44,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's effortless.

369
00:32:45,090 --> 00:32:47,930
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why ignorance means going with the grain.

370
00:32:48,550 --> 00:32:49,351
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the grain.

371
00:32:51,551 --> 00:32:53,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the direction of the stream.

372
00:32:53,071 --> 00:32:54,031
[SPEAKER_00]: So you go against it.

373
00:32:56,372 --> 00:32:57,512
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were to practice correctly.

374
00:32:58,752 --> 00:33:00,412
[SPEAKER_00]: You go against by holding it, what?

375
00:33:01,052 --> 00:33:04,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you go against it by full understanding, really.

376
00:33:05,433 --> 00:33:20,376
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no method or something, but full understanding that each domain, each sense, if that's what your approach is, means, yeah, it would be... well, it wouldn't be inducing the sense of self anymore.

377
00:33:20,916 --> 00:33:28,197
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, actually, the self that appears when a thing is seen twice in deflection,

378
00:33:29,708 --> 00:33:35,215
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the self of that thing.

379
00:33:35,535 --> 00:33:39,941
[SPEAKER_00]: The self-identity of the same thing that you hear and that you see.

380
00:33:40,582 --> 00:33:41,503
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's the same thing.

381
00:33:41,983 --> 00:33:42,664
[SPEAKER_00]: That sameness

382
00:33:43,370 --> 00:33:47,351
[SPEAKER_00]: You assume to be neither in what you see, nor in what you hear.

383
00:33:47,651 --> 00:33:48,732
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in me.

384
00:33:49,152 --> 00:33:50,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's the same self-think.

385
00:33:51,032 --> 00:33:53,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Myself is experiencing the sameness of these things.

386
00:33:54,474 --> 00:33:55,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Myself is.

387
00:33:55,674 --> 00:33:59,575
[SPEAKER_00]: Myself is independent of any other thing that I experience through my sense.

388
00:34:01,556 --> 00:34:02,816
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how it kind of goes.

389
00:34:06,858 --> 00:34:08,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I'm using senses to...

390
00:34:27,450 --> 00:34:39,138
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you will have to be able... which is what I mean when I talk about the principle of simultaneity in the notes of meditation.

391
00:34:40,179 --> 00:34:52,588
[SPEAKER_00]: When you simultaneously, here, see something, if your mindfulness is established properly, you'll be aware of it as two independent but simultaneous domains.

392
00:34:54,669 --> 00:34:55,930
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they don't actually meet.

393
00:34:56,617 --> 00:35:17,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Any of your senses, ever, on their own, they all kind of come together in your mind, but in their own domains, they're just like... Well, as I said in another essay, in regard to fire, indifferent to each other, it's just not even a... like, there is not even awareness in your hearing, in your hearing of your sight.

394
00:35:17,515 --> 00:35:18,415
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inconceivable.

395
00:35:19,316 --> 00:35:24,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Because having awareness of your sight in your hearing would be

396
00:35:25,713 --> 00:35:33,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, your sight appears in your hearing, even as a possibility, even as a different domain.

397
00:35:33,282 --> 00:35:34,183
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's inconceivable.

398
00:35:34,303 --> 00:35:35,064
[SPEAKER_00]: It just doesn't exist.

399
00:35:35,324 --> 00:35:39,409
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why you have five consciousnesses in regard to five senses and then six ones.

400
00:35:39,429 --> 00:35:41,011
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have six types of consciousness.

401
00:35:44,256 --> 00:35:45,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, when I...

402
00:35:45,077 --> 00:35:45,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Always.

403
00:35:45,457 --> 00:35:49,680
[SPEAKER_01]: When, if I see something, hear something, and then, and also touch and experience...

404
00:35:49,700 --> 00:35:52,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Imagine six layers of your experience.

405
00:35:52,602 --> 00:35:52,922
[SPEAKER_01]: But I...

406
00:35:52,942 --> 00:35:53,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Simultaneous.

407
00:35:53,602 --> 00:35:57,945
[SPEAKER_01]: It still seems like there is a paying different attention.

408
00:35:59,106 --> 00:36:00,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, from this center here.

409
00:36:01,147 --> 00:36:08,492
[SPEAKER_00]: From one... Yeah, because you give... you still have the assumption of me and myself as the core of your experience.

410
00:36:09,153 --> 00:36:12,535
[SPEAKER_00]: But you have to keep developing mindfulness as long as it's necessary.

411
00:36:13,499 --> 00:36:21,302
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, well, as I said before, when you feel that core, you can't start straight onto the senses now, the contemplation I'm talking about.

412
00:36:22,163 --> 00:36:23,043
[SPEAKER_00]: That can come later.

413
00:36:23,704 --> 00:36:24,504
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just too subtle.

414
00:36:25,704 --> 00:36:42,832
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now you would have to take up the experience as a whole and then include in that, that sense of core, that sense of center, which is sense of me and I am, as a phenomenon, as a peripheral phenomenon, without making it actual, all that we spoke before.

415
00:36:44,854 --> 00:36:48,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Because by including it, bit by bit, you become aware of it.

416
00:36:48,435 --> 00:37:04,100
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, the time will come when you recognize, well, if I'm aware of it, means it appears as a phenomenon, which means it will cease as a phenomenon, which means it can't possibly be me or anything related to me in any way.

417
00:37:08,202 --> 00:37:08,902
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a thought.

418
00:37:09,842 --> 00:37:11,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, just everything is just a thought.

419
00:37:13,177 --> 00:37:14,758
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a sight, it's an image.

420
00:37:16,159 --> 00:37:17,339
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, the sense of self.

421
00:37:17,499 --> 00:37:23,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's independent of the senses, which is how it has that assumed privileged position over that.

422
00:37:25,864 --> 00:37:39,352
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you stop and think, as we spoke before, this body, this physical body right here, which basically covers up the basis of the senses, the five senses, and then the experience, the general experience as a whole, in my sense of self or whatever else,

423
00:37:40,126 --> 00:37:45,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Would that be conceivable in any shape or form if this physical body is to be destroyed next instance?

424
00:37:46,150 --> 00:37:46,570
[SPEAKER_00]: It wouldn't.

425
00:37:47,230 --> 00:37:52,013
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the privileged position and attitude is slowly being undermined.

426
00:37:53,314 --> 00:37:54,314
[SPEAKER_00]: But you have to keep doing it.

427
00:37:57,016 --> 00:38:00,358
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you can... you're not even denying the sense of privilege of it.

428
00:38:00,398 --> 00:38:00,638
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

429
00:38:00,698 --> 00:38:01,479
[SPEAKER_00]: You include it.

430
00:38:02,239 --> 00:38:06,742
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you make sure it gets undermined with the right... with the right...

431
00:38:08,260 --> 00:38:11,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Settle with the right insight, with the right reflection.

432
00:38:14,826 --> 00:38:18,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why, in the ānāpānasati, I will breathe in like this.

433
00:38:19,750 --> 00:38:22,012
[SPEAKER_00]: I will breathe so you keep including yourself.

434
00:38:22,072 --> 00:38:25,735
[SPEAKER_00]: You keep including yourself in the next breath, in this breath.

435
00:38:25,936 --> 00:38:26,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, do it like this.

436
00:38:26,936 --> 00:38:34,143
[SPEAKER_00]: You're including your very volition and intention, while you're seeing clearly that even the most

437
00:38:35,358 --> 00:38:42,263
[SPEAKER_00]: The strongest willed thing, the most determined thing, well, could have never been yours to begin with.

438
00:38:44,004 --> 00:38:46,166
[SPEAKER_00]: There's simply no room for the for self.

439
00:38:47,386 --> 00:38:49,408
[SPEAKER_00]: And not just there's no room, it's not necessary at all.

440
00:38:50,068 --> 00:38:51,369
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why there is no room for it.

441
00:38:52,690 --> 00:38:55,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's a... you said that birth is intentional.

442
00:38:56,633 --> 00:39:02,397
[SPEAKER_01]: So you pay attention to the birth, you find that it's really intending.

443
00:39:03,283 --> 00:39:09,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can find that, but as long as you include your sense of self in regard to it.

444
00:39:09,967 --> 00:39:13,629
[SPEAKER_00]: What happens is, people would kind of forget about itself.

445
00:39:14,550 --> 00:39:16,331
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it would be, oh, breathing just happens.

446
00:39:17,532 --> 00:39:22,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's this thing breathing me, or it's this body breathing... All that is... you can say that.

447
00:39:22,835 --> 00:39:23,895
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not inaccurate.

448
00:39:24,216 --> 00:39:28,758
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that you left the sense of self out of it, means you're not really addressing the problem.

449
00:39:29,259 --> 00:39:31,200
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like getting the decent medicine,

450
00:39:32,223 --> 00:39:37,586
[SPEAKER_00]: But applying it over a covered-up wall, so it doesn't actually reach the point where it's supposed to be active.

451
00:39:38,426 --> 00:39:40,167
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you keep including your sense of self.

452
00:39:41,247 --> 00:39:42,708
[SPEAKER_00]: You keep bringing it forward.

453
00:39:43,449 --> 00:39:45,149
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not letting it kind of wiggle out.

454
00:39:46,650 --> 00:39:56,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is the same as the contemplation I described to you, when you recognize, okay, this body is right here, right now, in this situation, in this position, in this shape or form.

455
00:39:57,195 --> 00:39:58,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Fully, fully material.

456
00:39:59,898 --> 00:40:02,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, entirely composed of elements.

457
00:40:02,340 --> 00:40:07,163
[SPEAKER_00]: There's absolutely nothing in this body that doesn't pertain to one of the four great elements.

458
00:40:08,745 --> 00:40:23,756
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a hundred percent object, because of which you're thinking of what objects are, and that right reflection, while you're thinking, stands there, because of that random object, like that kettle, like that bottle, like that cup.

459
00:40:25,437 --> 00:40:28,159
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if something happens to it on that material level,

460
00:40:28,927 --> 00:40:36,350
[SPEAKER_00]: In which you have no access at all, you realize nothing can remain standing, even the greatest sense of self.

461
00:40:38,030 --> 00:40:50,295
[SPEAKER_00]: So, establish your mind upon that, and by establishing your mind upon that, I mean, in the beginning, you have to keep bringing it up, then you fall from it, you bring it up, but then you bring it up, and that thought, that perception of that relationship stays.

462
00:40:51,075 --> 00:40:52,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, ah, the mind is established to a degree.

463
00:40:53,296 --> 00:40:53,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Keep it.

464
00:40:54,596 --> 00:40:56,077
[SPEAKER_00]: When you lose it, bring it back.

465
00:41:23,025 --> 00:41:49,854
[SPEAKER_02]: Could one say that in recognizing that the sense of self is sort of derivative of like the different senses, sort of different senses pointing to that one focal point, and then recognizing that this impermanence of the sense is a way to get to realize that that sense of self, which is derived from it, is

466
00:41:51,749 --> 00:41:52,490
[SPEAKER_00]: That's solid.

467
00:41:53,530 --> 00:41:58,714
[SPEAKER_00]: Can be, for as long as you first acknowledge that focal point.

468
00:41:59,815 --> 00:42:01,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's real, as such, and it's there.

469
00:42:03,498 --> 00:42:15,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Because usually people would resort to contemplation of impermanence of a kind, to obscure, or to deny, or to cover up, or to remove that focal point.

470
00:42:17,856 --> 00:42:19,377
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the result.

471
00:42:20,418 --> 00:42:22,499
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's there, it means it has a reason.

472
00:42:22,999 --> 00:42:24,800
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever has a reason means it wants to arise.

473
00:42:25,860 --> 00:42:27,121
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no interference with that.

474
00:42:28,862 --> 00:42:43,609
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you were to contemplate impermanence like that, yeah, it will point out that this focal point is not... well, it cannot be taken as me and mine, because it's fully dependent upon these external organs.

475
00:42:44,109 --> 00:42:45,250
[SPEAKER_00]: They have no control over.

476
00:42:45,958 --> 00:42:51,219
[SPEAKER_00]: But that can become valid only after that focal point has been acknowledged and recognized as phenomena.

477
00:42:52,740 --> 00:42:56,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people often get too quick to say, there's no self.

478
00:42:57,481 --> 00:42:58,941
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no self, no self, no self.

479
00:42:59,461 --> 00:43:00,061
[SPEAKER_00]: There is self.

480
00:43:00,461 --> 00:43:01,342
[SPEAKER_00]: There is focal point.

481
00:43:01,582 --> 00:43:02,182
[SPEAKER_00]: It's on yours.

482
00:43:02,622 --> 00:43:03,582
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what no self is.

483
00:43:06,043 --> 00:43:07,463
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like throwing out the self.

484
00:43:07,523 --> 00:43:09,043
[SPEAKER_02]: You're throwing out the whole problem.

485
00:43:09,183 --> 00:43:09,403
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

486
00:43:09,424 --> 00:43:12,064
[SPEAKER_02]: Like a scientist, just pure objectivity.

487
00:43:12,084 --> 00:43:12,924
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

488
00:43:12,984 --> 00:43:15,505
[SPEAKER_00]: You're ignoring the problem.

489
00:43:17,387 --> 00:43:20,529
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I meant before when you asked about meditation the other day.

490
00:43:21,089 --> 00:43:22,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever arises, you include that.

491
00:43:23,770 --> 00:43:24,811
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same principle.

492
00:43:25,911 --> 00:43:27,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever arises, well, it has arisen.

493
00:43:28,892 --> 00:43:32,534
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, trying to exclude it is a mistake, just for that very reason.

494
00:43:33,635 --> 00:43:35,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you have no say over things arising.

495
00:43:36,396 --> 00:43:39,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you would, well, there would be a sense of self then.

496
00:43:40,258 --> 00:43:42,199
[SPEAKER_00]: But they manifest when they manifest.

497
00:43:43,026 --> 00:43:54,211
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you stop trying to interfere with that, if you recognize that whatever has a reason, you will see that for those very reasons of simple arising, that's why it will have to cease.

498
00:43:54,612 --> 00:43:55,652
[SPEAKER_00]: Not for anything else.

499
00:43:55,692 --> 00:43:56,853
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what impermanence is.

500
00:43:57,233 --> 00:44:00,374
[SPEAKER_00]: So impermanence actually is in arising of things.

501
00:44:00,735 --> 00:44:04,697
[SPEAKER_00]: Not, not like looking and waiting for their destruction to prove that they're impermanent.

502
00:44:05,677 --> 00:44:07,598
[SPEAKER_00]: No, they're impermanent because you're experiencing it.

503
00:44:08,581 --> 00:44:12,582
[SPEAKER_02]: And to recognize that whatever is arising, it's... it's complex.

504
00:44:13,162 --> 00:44:15,122
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like an integrated system.

505
00:44:15,462 --> 00:44:17,423
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything integrated has to disintegrate.

506
00:44:17,843 --> 00:44:18,723
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's not even that.

507
00:44:19,083 --> 00:44:21,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that's... that's another designation that you will put on top.

508
00:44:22,144 --> 00:44:24,064
[SPEAKER_00]: System integrated or simple or whatever.

509
00:44:26,424 --> 00:44:29,485
[SPEAKER_00]: The most accurate you can get is saying, it has arisen.

510
00:44:30,165 --> 00:44:30,745
[SPEAKER_00]: It is.

511
00:44:31,585 --> 00:44:32,005
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

512
00:44:33,286 --> 00:44:34,766
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the most accurate phenomenon.

513
00:44:36,500 --> 00:44:37,260
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't have to go further.

514
00:44:37,280 --> 00:44:46,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can't actually go further than that, because any other more elaborate explanation would be sort of going back down and then providing an explanation for it.

515
00:44:47,943 --> 00:44:50,943
[SPEAKER_00]: An explanation, even if it's a very pleasing one, is still an explanation.

516
00:44:52,324 --> 00:44:58,925
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it has to... it has to be recognized on that general level where it just arises.

517
00:44:59,005 --> 00:45:01,786
[SPEAKER_00]: And for that very reason, it's impermanent.

518
00:45:02,126 --> 00:45:02,906
[SPEAKER_00]: It will have to cease.

519
00:45:04,017 --> 00:45:08,138
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no say in its manifestation, which is why it will cease.

520
00:45:08,819 --> 00:45:09,219
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

521
00:45:10,679 --> 00:45:15,141
[SPEAKER_00]: When that's recognized, then everything, whether it's particular or general, is seen through that principle.

522
00:45:21,083 --> 00:45:27,725
[SPEAKER_02]: And the sense... and that... the very sense of self arises depending on... on these things that arise.

523
00:45:27,865 --> 00:45:31,987
[SPEAKER_00]: So then you recognize... because you recognize principles in regard to anything that manifests.

524
00:45:32,535 --> 00:45:34,036
[SPEAKER_00]: For that reason, it's impermanent.

525
00:45:35,537 --> 00:45:37,338
[SPEAKER_00]: You... well, yourself manifests.

526
00:45:38,639 --> 00:45:45,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Ambiguous, general, not specific, yet, it's manifested as such, thus, it's impermanent.

527
00:45:46,083 --> 00:45:55,008
[SPEAKER_00]: It can... with me, it doesn't even occur anymore, oh, this is my... it cannot even be assumed, even be thought of or fantasized anymore as myself.

528
00:45:55,288 --> 00:45:58,070
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just inconceivable, becomes, when that principle is understood.

529
00:46:00,267 --> 00:46:01,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is what Sister Vajira meant.

530
00:46:01,708 --> 00:46:04,469
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, she lost the dimension of her soul.

531
00:46:04,589 --> 00:46:17,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's just... Well, actually, she didn't quite lose it fully, back then, but she's clearly recognizing it, because if she lost it, she couldn't have even remembered it, in that sense.

532
00:46:17,996 --> 00:46:28,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can't even bring it back as a memory, because even the preview, the memory and the past is seen as impermanent, which means your self ceases to reside even there.

533
00:46:29,703 --> 00:46:30,984
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the dimension is gone.

534
00:46:31,144 --> 00:46:31,584
[SPEAKER_00]: It's gone.

535
00:46:31,964 --> 00:46:42,571
[SPEAKER_00]: So, Sotāpanna cannot even remember what... well, he can recognize what Atavāda is, assuming self out of the five aggregates, but he cannot even conceive that, even as a memory.

536
00:46:43,171 --> 00:46:44,652
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows, oh, I used to do this.

537
00:46:46,933 --> 00:46:56,379
[SPEAKER_00]: I used to, like, I used to think that I existed, independent of my senses, independent of my aggregates, but actually, how that

538
00:46:57,473 --> 00:46:59,634
[SPEAKER_00]: What's experienced actually, it's inconceivable.

539
00:47:00,554 --> 00:47:01,475
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot arise anymore.

540
00:47:03,376 --> 00:47:10,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why past and the future and the present get affected with the right view.

541
00:47:18,823 --> 00:47:21,084
[SPEAKER_02]: The real key is seeing, seeing how

542
00:47:22,155 --> 00:47:27,099
[SPEAKER_02]: This sense of self is depending upon these rising and falling phenomena.

543
00:47:27,259 --> 00:47:36,746
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the standard view for Kutujan, I think, is that I'm here, solid, and I'm experiencing all these things, and whether they come and go, I don't know.

544
00:47:36,826 --> 00:47:38,227
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... and that would be wrong.

545
00:47:38,247 --> 00:47:40,049
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what the Buddha meant by impermanence.

546
00:47:40,369 --> 00:47:45,933
[SPEAKER_00]: And the point is, no, the impermanence has to be personal, which means the impermanence has to pertain to that sense of self.

547
00:47:45,973 --> 00:47:46,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

548
00:47:46,694 --> 00:47:51,918
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're contemplating something that's coming and going, and you don't feel anything, that's... that's irrelevant.

549
00:47:52,480 --> 00:47:55,080
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to find the impermanence that will concern you.

550
00:47:55,941 --> 00:48:00,321
[SPEAKER_00]: Or rather, the thought of this thing disappearing will concern you.

551
00:48:00,902 --> 00:48:10,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is what I mean, impermanence, like if you... like seeing a thing manifest, and then seeing the destruction of the thing, it's actually another... another arising.

552
00:48:11,043 --> 00:48:16,484
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a manifestation, appearance, of a thing being... of a particular thing being disintegrated.

553
00:48:17,064 --> 00:48:18,685
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a impermanence.

554
00:48:18,765 --> 00:48:20,285
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not... it's actually more arising.

555
00:48:21,098 --> 00:48:29,867
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a worldly type of impermanence, in terms of things have changed their shape, or whatever else, but, terminologically speaking, it's just another arising experience.

556
00:48:32,089 --> 00:48:35,072
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't actually experience impermanence through your senses.

557
00:48:35,833 --> 00:48:39,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Any, in any way, you can't even conceive it, the true impermanence.

558
00:48:39,637 --> 00:48:43,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you would, you would need the Buddha to tell you how to find the way out of the self-view.

559
00:48:43,781 --> 00:48:44,342
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to

560
00:48:45,256 --> 00:48:48,959
[SPEAKER_00]: As most of the mystics believe, and hopeful, that's a sin.

561
00:48:48,979 --> 00:48:52,401
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, through a vision, through mental or whatever, and it's impossible.

562
00:48:54,623 --> 00:49:02,708
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of trying to see disappearing, you recognize that the appearance is the disease, because of which it's gone.

563
00:49:03,269 --> 00:49:05,310
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be gone, even if it isn't.

564
00:49:05,991 --> 00:49:11,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it is, that's another experience that has arisen, because it has arisen, the impermanence is impermanent.

565
00:49:13,396 --> 00:49:13,556
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

566
00:49:14,087 --> 00:49:21,417
[SPEAKER_00]: That which you regarded as permanent is also impermanent, and even the impermanent things that you regarded before are even more impermanent.

567
00:49:21,918 --> 00:49:28,707
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just impermanence and more impermanence, as opposed to impermanence and then too much of permanence.

